abstract
Fly larvae composting is an emerging waste treatment alternative with great potential to increase revenue from food waste management. For wider implementation, fly larvae composting has to be evaluated in comparison with conventional systems, based on direct greenhouse gas (GHG) emission data for the treatment process, which are currently limited. This study evaluated direct emissions of CO2, CH4, N2O and NH3 from composting of food waste using black soldier fly (BSF) larvae (Hermetia illucens). Use of BSF larvae-associated bacteria in 7-day pre-treatment and seeding at larvae treatment start were evaluated and compared to larvae treatment without bacteria addition. The treatments were performed in a set of 14-day laboratory-scale experiments. Mean substrate reduction was 49 ± 8% and bioconversion ratio was 24 ± 8% (both dry matter basis). Direct GHG emissions from the fly larvae treatment process were generally very small, with emissions of CH4 and N2O equivalent to 0.38 kg CO2-equivalents per ton food waste treated assuming global warming potential over 100 years, while mean total CO2 emissions were 96 g CO2 per kg food waste treated. Additional emissions could be expected to occur in the pre-treatment process, which did not provide any significant improvement in bioconversion ratio or reduction in total GHG emissions during treatment. Similarly, use of BSF larvae-associated bacteria did not significantly improve process efficiency. No NH3 emissions were detected, as reflected in total N mass balance over the treatment cycle. The results show that total direct GHG emissions from food waste treatment by fly larvae composting are lower than those from conventional food waste treatment, and that pre-treatment and seeding of food waste with BSF larvae-associated bacteria do not further reduce total GHG emissions. Ó 2019 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
1. Introduction
Fly larvae composting is a waste treatment method that has great potential to increase revenues from food waste management (Lalander et al., 2018). Fly larvae composting is an aerobic process (as is conventional composting) in which most of the energy available in the substrate is transformed into larval biomass (Lalander et al., 2015). This transformation is expected to result in lower CO2 emissions, as the part of carbon accumulated in larval biomass is not lost (Perednia, 2017). When a life cycle assessment (LCA) approach is used for evaluating the environmental impacts of fly larvae composting, data on direct greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions are needed, as the contribution of the treatment step must be included when estimating the global warming potential (GWP) of the whole system (Salomone et al., 2017). The larval biomass generated in fly larvae composting systems is a valuable additional
product to the fertiliser which is typically generated in biological treatment of food waste. Implementation of fly larvae composting allows implementation of a system where food waste is viewed as a resource and generates high value products unlike in conventional systems where at best a partial recovery of energy and plant nutrients is possible. The larval biomass resulting from fly larvae composting can be used as feed for animals, further reducing the overall system emissions since other sources of feed (e.g. soybean) can be replaced (Allegretti et al., 2018). Other potential emissions from the degradation process, such as methane (CH4) and nitrous oxide (N2O), may be a concern, as they are in conventional food waste treatment methods such as composting and anaerobic digestion (Møller et al., 2009; Pipatti et al., 2006). Emissions of CH4 and N2O are relevant to investigate, as they have GWP of 34 and 289, respectively, over 100 years (IPCC, 2013). Studies focusing on LCA of waste management systems and investigating fly larva composting as one of the alternatives highlighted knowledge gaps in inventory data on emissions from fly larvae composting as a food waste treatment (Allegretti et al.,
