2018; Mertenat et al., 2019; Salomone et al., 2017). These LCA studies use emission data for other insect species to overcome the lack of data. A recent LCA study by Mertenat et al. (2019) evaluated direct emissions from BSF treatment of food waste and found that they were lower than in conventional composting. However, further evaluation based on homogeneous feedstock and carbon and nitrogen balance is necessary to evaluate the effect of substrate and process parameters on the emissions. To our knowledge, direct CH4 or N2O emissions from fly larvae composting of food waste have not been determined to date under controlled laboratory conditions. Emissions of CH4 typically occur due to methanogenic microbe activity under anaerobic conditions and can be expected to be low in fly larvae composting, due to larval movement mechanically turning the substrate and making it more aerobic (Cickova et al., 2015). Emissions of N2O occur from processes such as nitrification and denitrification in aerobically degraded organic material and depend on both substrate properties and microbial activity (Ermolaev et al., 2015). Overall low N2O emissions have been reported for Neomyia cornicina fly larvae treating cattle dung, an effect attributed to inhibition of microbial degradation activity and nitrogen assimilation within larval biomass (Iwasa et al., 2015),. Fly larvae of Hermetia illucens have been reported to modify the microbiome of their surrounding substrate (Jeon et al., 2011; Varotto Boccazzi et al., 2017). However, the effects of this on emissions of N2O have not been investigated. Another relevant gas associated with aerobic degradation of organic material is ammonia (NH3), as it contributes to acidification and eutrophication (IPCC, 2014). This gas can represent a major pathway of nitrogen losses in conventional composting (Jarvis et al., 2009). Ammonia has been reported to cause odours and even to create a hazardous environment for workers in fly larvae composting and can represent a major pathway for nitrogen losses from the compost produced (Cickova et al., 2015; Cickova et al., 2012; Lalander et al., 2015). The pathway for NH3 emissions can be expected to be similar to that in conventional composting. Several factors are decisive for NH3 emissions during composting; when aerobic degradation occurs at a high rate, organic nitrogen mineralisation produces ammonium (NH+4) as the primary product (Paredes et al., 1996). Ammonium is converted to gaseous form (NH3) when the substrate pH shifts towards alkaline and the rate of NH3 emission increases with aeration and temperature (Rynk and Richard, 2001). High efficiency in fly larva composting on optimal substrates makes this method attractive for use as a major food waste treatment method (Lalander et al., 2019). However, further process improvement is needed, as organic degradation in the process is not complete (Lalander et al., 2018). BSF larvae-associated bacteria have been suggested as a means to improve the efficiency in the fly larvae composting process (Yu et al., 2011). Pre-treatment of the substrate with these bacteria could potentially improve nutrient availability to the larvae, as parts of food waste are otherwise slow to degrade (Yu et al., 2011; Zheng et al., 2013). Alternatively, seeding the substrate with BSF-associated bacteria at the start of larvae treatment could accelerate the process of larval microbiome adjustment, while also stimulating degradation to some degree (Xiao et al., 2018; Yu et al., 2011). The aim of this study was to evaluate GHG and NH3 emissions from fly larvae composting treatment and the effects of pre-treatment and seeding of substrate with BSF larvaeassociated bacteria on the efficiency and rate of the fly larvae composting process and the amount of emissions produced. The results of this study are key in performing further life cycle assessment (LCA) evaluation of fly larvae composting as a waste management alternative and for comparing its performance to other waste treatment alternatives, currently there is lack of such data.
2. Material and methods
2.1. Materials
In all treatments performed in this study, food waste was used as the substrate for the larvae. This food waste was sourced from a local canteen at SLU (Uppsala, Sweden) and represented a mixture of food preparation waste, food scraps and unsold prepared food. The food waste had a dry matter (DM) content of 24.9 ± 2.9% of wet weight (WW), volatile solids content of 92.3 ± 0.5% of DM, pH of 4.42 ± 0.63 and density of 1.09 ± 0.06 g mL 1 on average (n = 3). The collected substrate was homogenised, divided into feeding portions and stored at 20 °C until use, prior to which it was thawed and allowed to reach room temperature for up to 12 h. In all treatments, 5-d-old, >0.2 cm long BSF larvae obtained from a colony continuously running since 2015 at SLU (Uppsala, Sweden) were used.
2.2. Study layout
All treatments were performed in 11 cm deep small boxes with dimensions of 21 17 cm giving a treatment surface of 357 cm2 to which 700 larvae were added, giving a larval density of 2 larvae cm 2. The larvae density and feeding rate of 30.0 ± 0.3 mg dry matter larva 1 d 1 were selected to ensure sufficient nutrition and avoid overcrowding in accordance to previously established recommendations (Diener et al., 2011). The boxes were incubated at 28 °C during the 14-day treatment period to obtain larvae in the fifth instar. The larvae were added by weight based on individual larvae weight determined on a smaller sample. Three treatments were compared: Treatment L was standard fly larvae composting, where only fly larvae were added to the food waste; in treatment BL, fly larvae and a spore-forming bacteria consortium isolated from the gut of BSF larvae were added at the start of the composting; in treatment PBL, the food waste was pre-treated in open boxes at 28 °C for 7 days with the same bacteria consortium as in BL, after which fly larvae were added (Fig. 1). Spore-forming bacteria were chosen due to simplicity of their implementation as inoculum in future applications but also to avoid possibility of recycling pathogens. At the end of the experiments, the larvae were picked out, weighed and enumerated. Based on the final number of larvae, their survival was calculated in relation to the initial number. The treatment residues were weighed and sampled for analysis of dry matter content (DM), total volatile solids (VS) and total nitrogen (N). All treatments were performed in triplicate.
2.3. Bacterial isolation
Twelve BSF larvae (BSFL) reaching instar 5 (reared in food waste) were picked out and washed in deionised water and then in disinfection solution (70% EtOH). The disinfected larvae were manually chopped to pieces and immersed in 10 mL (4 BSFL/10 mL) nutrient broth (1 g/L Lab-Lemco powder, 2 g/L yeast extract, 5 g/L peptone and 5 g/L NaCl, National Veterinary Institute, Uppsala, Sweden) at 70 °C for 10 min. After cooling to room temperature (RT), 1 mL of broth from each tube was spread on taurocholate gelatine agar (TGA) plates (£ 14 cm; Miclev, Sweden) and the plates were incubated at 37 °C for 24 h. Five visually different colonies were clean-spread on TGA plates and incubated at 37 °C for 24 h. Single individual colonies were taken and heattreated once more in the nutrient broth (as described above). For each colony, upon reaching RT, 10 mL of broth were spread on a TGA plate and the plates were incubated at 37 °C for 24 h. Once again, single colonies were collected from each plate and cleanspread on TGA plates that were incubated at 37 °C for 24 h. The
