2.4. Material sampling
Substrate samples were collected from the initial food waste mixture and from residues left at the end of the treatments (Fig. 1). These were analysed gravimetrically for DM content by drying at 80 °C until stable weight and for VS content by incineration at 250 °C for 2 h, followed by 550 °C for 4 h. Homogenised food waste density was estimated by filling graduated cylinders and measuring the volume and weight of the substrate. For total nitrogen (total N) determination in food waste, 0.5 g of material was boiled in concentrated sulphuric acid (H2SO4), following the procedure described in Lalander et al. (2015). The samples were neutralised using 10 M sodium hydroxide (NaOH), diluted 1:50 in deionised water and digested using SpectroquantÒ Crack-Set 20 (1.14963.0001). The nitrate concentration in the digested solution was then determined at 340 nm using the SpectroquantÒ nitrate test (1.09713.0002). Larvae total N was analysed externally (AgriLab AB, Sweden) in dried ground samples using a modified SSISO 13878 method and LECO CN-200 analyser. Treatment residue total N was analysed in the same way as larvae, but an additional NH4-N measurement was performed on a wet sample using a FOSS TECATOR FIAstar 5000 Analyzer and following a modified method supplied in instrument application notes (AN 65/84, ASN 01/92 and ASN 02/92) and added to the total N value. The maturity of treatment residues was measured after adjusting its DM content to 28% (on average) by addition of dry sawdust (93% DM), using the SolvitaÒ test following the procedure provided by the supplier.
2.5. Gas sampling
Emissions of GHG (CO2, CH4 and N2O) and of NH3 were sampled using a closed chamber technique. Each treatment box and a
battery-driven fan (Rubicson, Kjell & Co Elektronik AB, Sweden) were placed in a chamber closed with an airtight lid, equipped with a sampling port and pressure normalisation loop tube (1 m long, £ 4 mm) for approximately 60 min. Frequency of gas sampling (Fig. 1) was selected to balance the ability to perform multiple treatments in triplicates and provide a sufficient resolution for gas emission dynamics. The gas samples were taken at intervals of 0, 15, 30 and 60 min using a 60 mL syringe and transferred to gastight N-evacuated 20 mL glass vials in pairs, which were later analysed by gas chromatography (GC) (Clarus 500, Perkin Elmer, USA) for CO2, CH4 and N2O following the procedure described in Ermolaev et al. (2015). At each interval, after taking the two gas samples for GC analysis, NH3 gas concentration was measured directly by connecting the opening of a NH3 gas detection tube (105SD, 105SC, Kitagawa, Japan) to the sampling port and extracting the sample using a Kitagawa gas pump (AP-20, Kitagawa, Japan). The volume of extracted gas was recorded, as it resulted in chamber gas dilution through the pressure normalisation tube. This was compensated for by calculating the gas concentration using the known concentrations in the sample and room air and the known volumes of the chamber and extracted samples, described as:
