3. Results
3.1. Material reduction and bioconversion ratios
The conversion efficiency was similar for all treatments, but with a slightly greater average mass reduction (52.3 ± 4.2) and larvae bioconversion ratio (27.9 ± 3.7) in the bacteria and larvae (BL) treatment (Table 1). However, there was high variation between replicates and no significant difference between treatments was found. Similarly, no significant difference between the treatments was found for any of the parameters analysed. The residues remaining after the treatments had a DM content of 22% on average, which was lower than that of the initial food waste (25%) (Table 1). Larval size differed slightly between the treatments, but the differences were not significant (Table 1). Larval survival estimation was based on initial number of larvae extrapolated from a smaller sample and the final number of larvae enumerated in their totality. The final number of larvae exceeded the initial estimation by 22% in one of the replicates and by 1% in another in treatment BL. In those cases, the survival was assumed to be 100% and the initial larvae numbers were adjusted to 856 and 710, respectively, bringing the average survival in that treatment to 98.3%. With such an adjustment, the larval survival in treatment BL was significantly higher than in treatment PBL, although with a relatively high P value of 0.05 (Table 1). Residue maturity was low across the treatments, with a compost maturity index (CMI) of 2.3 on average (n = 9), indicating that
the residues could be classified as very active compost (Table 1). While CMI is calculated based on SolvitaÒ measurements of CO2 and NH3 emissions, in our treatments these measurements generally showed no detectable NH3 (n = 7) and high CO2 concentrations (data not shown). Overall, the majority of available DM and VS remained in the products after the treatment, and 67–78% of initial dry solids and VS available in the substrate was recovered in the larvae and residues after treatment (Table 2). Most of the water initially available in the material was recovered in the residues (Table 2), resulting in relatively low residue DM (Table 1). Larvae contained 10–17% of the initial water, while 18–31% of water was lost during the treatment (cf. Table 2). As water is also generated in the process of respiration, it was possible to estimate the amount of water produced based on the VS loss. On average, 64.3 g VS was lost during the larvae composting in treatments L, BL and PBL, while an additional 30.3 g VS was lost, on average, in the pre-treatment of PBL (cf. Table 2). Based on the oxidation reaction of the average food waste formula (Haug, 1993) it was estimated that, on average, 34.2 g of water were generated during the larvae composting step in all treatments and an additional 16.1 g during pre-treatment of PBL due to VS loss in respiration. Larvae contained a higher amount of N (60.7 mg N g 1 DM, on average) than the food waste substrate (Table 2), while the residues contained a lower amount. However, from a mass balance perspective, over a half the initial N ended up in larvae and the rest was recovered in the residues (Table 2). Total N in all treatment products was close to 100% of the initial N, suggesting high N recovery, however most likely also caused by variation between the replicates and possible underestimation of initial N amount in the food waste. Variation associated with analytical methods and process development in pre-treatments and treatments in some cases resulted in overestimation in N balance due to counteractive combinations of such variations.
3.2. Gas emissions
The CO2 emission rates and patterns were similar during the larvae composting step in the different treatments. The CO2 emission rate peaked during the second measurement on day 7 of fly larvae composting, reaching 7.4 mg min 1 on average across the treatments, and then declined slightly by day 11 to 5.6 mg min 1 (Fig. 2A). Total emissions of CO2 were similar across all treatments, excluding any emissions from pre-treatment (Fig. 2B). Total emissions of CO2 in the larvae treatment step were on average 96.5 g CO2 kg 1 initial food waste on a wet weight (WW) basis (Fig. 2B, Table 3).
