The CO2 emissions during the pre-treatment step in the PBL treatment were estimated from VS loss as suggested by Haug (1993), assuming a carbon fraction of 55% of VS. This resulted in an additional 51.5 ± 13.7 g CO2 kg 1 initial food waste (WW) being produced on average in the pre-treatment step, assuming that all the carbon in the lost VS was converted to CO2. In all three treatments, the CH4 emission rates were relatively low and they generally declined from start to end of larvae composting (Fig. 2C). Initial CH4 emissions at the beginning of larvae composting in treatment PBL were lower on average than in the experiments without pre-treatment and were closer to CH4 emission rates measured on day 11 of those experiments (Fig. 2C). This difference was not significant for any of the sampling days, but when comparing the emission rates grouped by day and treatment, the CH4 emission rates in treatment PBL were significantly lower than those in treatment BL (P = 0.005). The lower CH4 emission rates resulted in lower overall total CH4 emissions (Fig. 2D, Table 3). Total emissions differed between treatments and were significantly lower in the larvae composting step of the PBL treatment with pre-treated substrate (P = 0.02) than in treatment BL, where bacteria and larvae treated the substrate (Fig. 2D). The emissions did not differ significantly between treatments L and BL or between treatments L and PBL (Fig. 2D). The CH4 emissions were not measured during the pre-treatment step and were not included in the comparisons, but they could potentially increase the total CH4 emissions from the whole PBL treatment process. The N2O emissions did not show any specific pattern and the majority of the values measured were very close to background levels (Fig. 2E). The flux values were often assumed to be zero, as the interval measurements in the chamber produced low R2 values (R2 < 0.9, n = 9 of 27) due to both initial N2O concentration close to background levels and no concentration increase in the chamber over the measuring period. The N2O results presented here are thus an estimate of the actual emissions. The estimated N2O emission rates varied between replicates and thus the mean values had relatively high standard deviation (Table 3). The emission rates during larvae composting in treatment PBL were so low that they were not significantly different from zero (Fig. 2E). This resulted in cumulative N2O emissions in
this treatment being very small, and the standard deviation being comparable to the mean of the replicates (Fig. 2F, Table 3). Overall, cumulative N2O emissions from the larvae composting step were not significantly different between the three treatments and showed large variation. The N2O emissions from the pretreatment step in treatment PBL were not measured. Ammonia emissions were below the detection limit of 0.2 ppm in all cases (n = 27).
4. Discussion
4.1. Process efficiency
Bioconversion ratio and substrate reduction were not significantly different between the treatments, as evidenced by the similarity in total CO2 emissions between the treatments, indicating that a similar amount of the VS content in the substrate was converted to larval biomass (Table 1, Fig. 2A-B). Mean estimated total CO2 emissions in all treatments were 96.5 g CO2 kg 1 initial food waste (WW basis), corresponding to a loss of 53.8 g VS (based on carbon conversion), which was less than the overall average of 64.3 g VS estimated to be lost from the mass balance (Table 2). This difference could be explained by either underestimation of the total CO2 emissions, emissions of other volatile substances not measured in this study or inaccuracy in VS measurements. Overall, after larvae composting in all treatments, approximately half of the initial VS was recovered in the residues (Table 2). The relationship between ash, VS and DM only changed by 2–5 percentage units, from around 92 to 98%, as some minerals were taken up by the larvae together with VS. The high proportion of initial VS remaining in the residues suggests that, even after larval composting in all treatments, the residues could still be considered only mildly degraded (Table 2). This was confirmed by the low SolvitaÒ CMI value obtained for the residues (Table 1). This is typical for fly larvae composting of food waste, as BSF larvae grow quickly and thus consume the most easily digestible parts of the substrate (Li et al., 2011; Li et al., 2015). The part of VS converted by the larvae (representing bioconversion ratio on a VS basis) was 21 and 28% of initial VS in treatments L and BL, respectively (Table 1),
