optimal. The effect of the feeding rate is e.g. seen in the study by Lalander et al. (2019) where a lower bioconversion ratio of 13.9% of DM was achieved for food waste with higher feeding rate of 40 mg DM larvae 1 d 1, suggesting that a feeding rate of 30.0 mg DM larva 1 d 1 selected in current study resulted in a better conversion efficiency. Overall, the nitrogen balance indicated that, of the initial N in the food waste, over a half was recovered in the larvae and the rest in the treatment residues (Table 2). In treatment L, 97% of the initial N in the substrate was recovered in these two fractions, while in treatment BL 100% was recovered. This indicates small N losses from the system. In treatment PBL, the high N recovery in the two fractions produced suggests that there was no major N loss either during the pre-treatment step or during larvae composting. Two major factors were identified as reasons for the very high N content in the fractions produced in relation to the initial N in all treatments (Table 2). The primary factor was probably underestimation of total N value in the incoming food waste, as the initial sampling may not have reflected the total composition of the material due to unexpected inhomogeneity of incoming material. In fact, another study performed on food waste from the same source reported a higher initial N value (49.5 mg N g 1 DM; unpublished data) than found here (35.4 mg N g 1 DM; Table 2). The second factor was deviation in substrate analysis (the method has a 10% error). The N content of the larvae has been shown to be consistent within a certain range for BSF larvae and does not depend strongly on initial N, as long as it is within an acceptable range for their growth (Lalander et al., 2019).
4.2. Gas emissions
The CO2 emissions were similar between the treatments throughout the study and showed a similar pattern, with higher CO2 emission rate measured on day 7 of the experiments and a slight decrease on day 11 (Fig. 2A). This emissions pattern differs from that in the thermophilic composting process, where an initial peak is typically observed early in the microbial degradation process, when easily available substances are consumed by the proliferating microorganisms (Ermolaev et al., 2015). A second peak associated with process temperature increase if the compost becomes thermophilic, and with more substances becoming easily degradable at such temperatures, can be expected (Ermolaev et al., 2015). In fly larvae composting, the substrate development had a lower effect on the CO2 emissions (Fig. 2A-B). Furthermore, in treatment PBL with pre-treated substrate, there was no significant difference in CO2 produced during larvae treatment compared with the other two treatments, suggesting the larvae themselves are the major source of emissions, since the bacteria introduced in pretreatment (PBL) and seeded at start of the treatment (BL) would contribute to CO2 emissions due to their respiration in addition to that of larvae themselves (Table 3). The near linearity in cumulative CO2 emissions could also be expected, as the larvae grow in a nearly linear way (Lalander et al., 2019) and thus their respiration could be expected to increase accordingly (Banks, 2014). No strong correlation (R2 = 0.53) between CO2 emission rates and larvae size was observed in our experiments, but it was significant (P < 0.0001) and in the cases where larvae were small, the emission rates were also smaller, while the CO2 flux was never below 0.003 g min 1 after the larvae reached a size of 0.2 g (Fig. 3). This means that while there was a significant correlation with larvae size, it could not explain all the variance in CO2 production rate. Larger variation in CO2 emission rates later in the process can be related to multiple factors, including the establishment of a microbial community, resulting in a larger contribution of microbial res
piration to total CO2 emissions and differences in larval survival between treatments and replicates (Table 1). The CH4 emissions dynamics suggested that during the process of larvae treatment, the material becomes less anaerobic, thus reducing CH4 production rates during larvae composting (Fig. 2C). There was only a weak correlation (R2 = 0.43, 0.42 and 0.38 for treatments L, BL and PBL, respectively) between CH4 emission rates and treatment time, mostly due to variation in CH4 flux measured in different replicates (Fig. 2C–D). However, parts of the substrate remained anaerobic as CH4 production continued, although at much slower rate on days 7 and 11 (Fig. 2C–D). In aerobic composting, the CH4 emissions typically increase 4–10 days later in the process, due to a combination of factors, including a longer time required for methanogen population growth and thermophilic temperatures (Ermolaev et al., 2015; Ermolaev et al., 2012; Jäckel et al., 2005). In our fly larvae composting treatments the opposite was observed. The reduction in CH4 emissions with treatment time could be associated with larvae growth, as they turn material more efficiently when they are larger and thus aerate it more (Cickova et al., 2015). Overall, the total CH4 emissions from the treatment period were similar between the two treatments without pre-treatment, with no significant difference, while these emissions were significantly smaller in treatment PBL compared with BL (Table 3). The total CH4 emissions were very small, one-thousandth of the level observed during conventional composting (Ermolaev et al., 2012). This likely relates to the majority of the process being aerobic, performed in thin layers and constantly turned. When compared on a carbon basis, the total CH4 emissions represented 3.3 g of VS loss (5.3% of total VS), only a fraction of the carbon emissions compared with CO2, which was calculated to be responsible for losses of 53.8 g VS (87%) on average during all treatments. The CH