emissions in treatment PBL to 1.63 ± 0.70 mg CH4 kg 1 FW, a value still lower than in the other treatments (Table 3). The results obtained indicate very low (or no) N2O and NH3 emissions, as also indicated by the total N recovery in the N balance (Table 2, Fig. 2E–F). A lack of detectable NH3 emissions was confirmed by the SolvitaÒ measurements (Table 1). The lowest reading, 4.5 out of 5 units, was detected only from residues in one out of nine replicates in all treatments. In a study by Lalander et al. (2015), NH3 emissions from fly larvae composting were detected (olfactory identification) and confirmed through N balance, indicating a loss of up to 50% of initial N through emissions to air. Moreover, that study reported higher pH in the residues, which would enhance the NH3 emissions. The low NH3 emissions in the present study could be dependent on several factors. First, the final residue pH was 5.1 on average (Table 1). Second, there may be a lack of NH+4 in the residues, as also observed by Green and Popa (2012), which is likely due to larval assimilation of N-containing molecules such as proteins. On the other hand, Cickova et al. (2015) suggest that in fly larvae composting, substantial NH3 emissions can be expected from the increasingly aerobic process due to larval movement in the substrate. Those authors also suggest that the material can dry out or warm up and the pH of the residues can become alkaline, which was not the case in the present study (see Table 1). Third, other studies reporting high ammonia emissions from fly larvae treatment have used manure as substrate (Cickova et al., 2012; Iñiguez-Covarrubias et al., 1994), and manure can be expected to contain higher concentrations of degraded N forms and generally have a higher pH. These may be associated with microbial degradation of remaining organic N and other forms of N excreted by larvae due to their metabolism, such as uric acid (Bursell, 1967). The importance of moisture and pH changes should be investigated in future experiments, to extend knowledge on NH3 emission dynamics during fly larvae composting. In conventional composting, it has been reported that factors such as process temperature becoming thermophilic and pH increasing above 6 are responsible for the NH3 emissions (Ermolaev et al., 2015). The residues in all treatments were not stabilised during larvae composting (Table 1) and the SolvitaÒ test indicated stability similar to that of fresh compost. Thus the treatment residues require an additional treatment step for stabilisation. Previous studies have shown that Salmonella spp. and viruses (reoviruses and adenoviruses) are inactivated in the larvae composting process, but not parasites (Ascaris suum) (Lalander et al., 2013; Lalander et al., 2015). During additional treatment steps to stabilise and sanitise the residue, there is a risk of additional emissions, and thus the optimal conditions for emissions reduction have to be maintained and emissions monitored. In a full-scale environmental assessment, management of the residues needs to be included.
4.3. Perspectives
Fly larvae composting not only performs a valuable function in the form of organic waste management, but also generates a valuable product in the form of larval biomass (Lalander et al., 2018). When the emissions from a waste treatment alternative are being compared, a comprehensive systems analysis should be performed, where results from multiple studies are incorporated. Such a study should consider the value of larvae biomass as the product. In such cases, emissions per unit mass of larvae and per ton of dry feed replacement produced can be estimated (Table 4). This study focused on the direct emissions from the treatment step. The CO2 equivalents of emissions of CH4 and N2O were 1/1000 of the actual CO2 emissions when expressed per ton of dry larval biomass produced (Table 4). Treatment PBL produced less CH4 and N2O than the other two treatments, but total emissions of CO2 nearly doubled, which resulted in significantly higher total emissions per ton DM of larvae produced including CO2, CH4 and N2O in treatment PBL comparing to L and BL (P < 0.00005). However, this did not result in a significant increase in bioconversion ratio, indicating that such pre-treatment is unnecessary (Tables 2 and 5). A similar conclusion can be drawn when looking at emissions per 1000 larvae also resulting in significantly higher (P < 0.0005) total emissions in treatment PBL comparing to L and BL (Table 4). A complete system evaluation should thus complement the results obtained in this study with other relevant impacts and when other feeds are replaced with larvae produced. Evaluation of direct emissions from fly larvae composting enables comparison of this treatment with treatment steps in other common organic waste treatment alternatives, such as composting and anaerobic digestion (Table 5). Emissions of CO2 during fly larvae composting are of biogenic origin and may be considered non
