contributing to global climate change (Bernstad and Jansen, 2012). In such an interpretation of total GHG emissions from fly larvae composting, the total emissions are very small, only around 0.38 kg CO2-eq. ton 1 food waste treated (Table 3). A recent study by Mertenat et al. (2019) on emissions of CH4 and N2O from larger crate fly larvae composting reported lower total CH4 and higher N2O emissions (Table 5). However, the N2O emissions pattern was different from that in the present study, with emission peaks appearing after feeding events (Mertenat et al., 2019). In the present study, the gas measurements were performed less frequently and before the feeding events, to avoid overestimation of the emissions associated with system disturbance due to feed addition. Similar N2O emission peaks to those appearing directly after feed addition have been observed in composting, at the start of the process, if the food waste contains nitrate (Ermolaev et al., 2015). This suggests that further investigation may be necessary, with more frequent gas measurements and nitrate content evaluation of incoming food waste. Organic waste treatment alternatives often include several process steps that may contribute additional direct GHG emissions to the overall system total. In anaerobic digestion, additional emissions can be expected due to biofilter emissions and digestate storage and during upgrading and combustion of biogas (Møller et al., 2009). Since an additional treatment step is necessary for fly larvae composting residues, additional NH3 or N2O emissions can be expected if the residues are still relatively moist, similarly to possible emissions during the curing stage of the composting process (Ermolaev et al., 2012). The emissions from fly larvae composting residue posttreatment have not yet been evaluated. Direct and indirect emissions during all the process stages for fly larvae composting have to be performed to allow fair comparisons.
5. Conclusions
Overall GHG emissions from fly larvae composting of food waste using BSF larvae were comparatively low. CO2 from food waste degradation was the major GHG emitted, with total emissions of 96 g CO2 per kg food waste treated. Emissions of CH4 and N2O were equivalent to 0.38 kg CO2-equivalents per ton food waste treated (assuming GWP over 100 years). No NH3 emissions were detected, which was reflected in total N mass balance over the treatment cycle. There was no significant improvement in material reduction or bioconversion ratio when food waste was seeded with larvaeassociated bacteria, compared with food waste treatment with larvae alone. Moreover, there was no significant effect of different treatments on total GHG emissions.
